\).  \n\n*Horizontal replacement.*  Use the line \\(y=b\\).  \n*Vertical replacement.*  Use the line \\(x=a\\).  \n*Diagonal replacement.*  Use the line \\(x+y=a+b\\).\n\nAll three families contain infinitely many distinct lines, so we can perform the replacement without creating coincidences with lines already present.\n\nIf we replace exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines, the number of sunny lines becomes  \n\n\\[\nk=(n-2)-t,\n\\]\n\nwhile the total number of lines stays \\(n\\) and every point of \\(S_{n}\\) remains covered (the replacement line still contains the point that motivated the original sunny line).\n\nConsequently **every integer**\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2\n\\]\nis realizable **provided** the replacement does not force two different points to share the same replacement line.  \nBecause the three families are parallel, two distinct points can lie on the same replacement line only if they are already collinear in that family.  \nIn our construction the points \\((a,b)\\) that we replace are all distinct and belong to different rows, columns and diagonals (otherwise they would have coincident slopes, contradicting the injectivity proved in §3.3).  \nHence we can replace them arbitrarily, and the above range of \\(k\\) is achievable **for all \\(n\\) except possibly when the set \\(R_{n}\\) is too small to allow the required number of replacements**.\n\n---\n\n### 3.5  The exceptional case \\(n=4\\)\n\nFor \\(n=4\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nR_{4}= \\{(2,2),\\;(2,3),\\;(3,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line through two of these points is either horizontal, vertical or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is **non‑sunny**.  \nHence at least **three** sunny lines are needed to cover the three points of \\(R_{4}\\).  \n\nOur construction of §3.3 would give \\(n-2=2\\) sunny lines, which is impossible.  \nIndeed, with the mandatory two non‑sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) we can have at most two sunny lines, leaving at least one point of \\(R_{4}\\) uncovered.  \n\nThus the pair \\((n,k)=(4,2)\\) cannot occur.\n\nAll other values \\(k\\le n-2\\) are attainable for \\(n=4\\):\n* \\(k=0\\): take the four horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3,4\\);\n* \\(k=1\\): take \\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=5\\) (non‑sunny) and one sunny line through \\((2,2)\\);\n* \\(k=3\\): use the construction of §3.3 (which gives three sunny lines) and replace one of them by a non‑sunny line.\n\n---\n\n### 3.6  Completion of the proof  \n\n* Lemma 3.2.1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n* §3.3 shows that the maximal value \\(k=n-2\\) is always attainable except when \\(n=4\\).  \n* §3.4 proves that every smaller non‑negative integer down to \\(0\\) can be obtained by replacing sunny lines with non‑sunny ones, and the replacement never creates coincidences.  \n* §3.5 treats the unique obstruction for \\(n=4\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one stated in the boxed classification. ∎\n\n\n\n--- \n\n## 4.  Summary of the defense  \n\n* The earlier construction “one sunny line per point of \\(R_{n}\\)” was indeed incorrect; it produced too many lines and relied on a false injectivity claim.  \n* The revised proof replaces that construction by a **single sunny line through each point of \\(R_{n}\\) and the fixed point \\((1,1)\\)**.  \n* The injectivity of the slopes \\(\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\) for points of \\(R_{n}\\) is proved rigorously, guaranteeing exactly \\(|R_{n}|\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n* Counting shows that together with the two fixed non‑sunny lines we obtain precisely \\(n\\) lines and \\(n-2\\) sunny ones.  \n* A systematic replacement argument yields all smaller values of \\(k\\); the only obstruction appears for \\(n=4\\), where the geometry of \\(R_{4}\\) forces at least three sunny lines.  \n\nThus the corrected answer and proof are now fully rigorous and immune to the previously raised objections."
