\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{t=0}^{\\,n-1}(t+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A bound for a sunny line  \n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line and denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(L)=|L\\cap S_n|\n\\]\n\nthe number of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nFor every sunny line \\(L\\) one has  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(L)\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor a point \\((x,y)\\in L\\cap S_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nx+y=(1+m)x+c .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\neq-1\\), the integer \\(1+m\\) is non‑zero; let  \n\n\\[\nd=|1+m|\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\]\n\nEquation (3) shows that the values of the sum \\(x+y\\) taken on the integer points of \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression with difference \\(d\\).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\); therefore the progression can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{d}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{d}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n\\]\n\ndifferent integers.  \nBecause \\(d\\ge1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(L)\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{1}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n        =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Even numbers of sunny lines are impossible  \n\nAssume that for some integer \\(k\\ge1\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_k\\).  \nUsing Lemma 1 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|S_k|\n   \\le k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, say \\(k=2m\\), then \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=m\\) and (4) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2m(2m+1)}2\\le 2m\\cdot 