es vertical, e.g. \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).)\n\n4. **Odd \\(k\\) is always possible.**  \n   Write \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \n   Consider the three families of sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\mathcal A_i &: 2x+y=2m+3+i\\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,m),\\\\[2mm]\n   \\mathcal B_i &: x+2y=2m+3+i\\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,m-1),\\\\[2mm]\n   \\mathcal C   &: y=x .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *All lines in (3) are sunny* (slopes \\(-2\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(1\\)).  \n   The family \\(\\mathcal A\\) consists of \\(m+1\\) lines, \\(\\mathcal B\\) of \\(m\\) lines and \\(\\mathcal C\\) is a single line; altogether we have exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (m+1)+m+1-1=2m+1=k\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines (the line \\(\\mathcal A_m\\) contains only the point \\((2m+1,1)\\) and this point also lies on \\(\\mathcal B_0\\); therefore we may omit \\(\\mathcal A_m\\) and keep the remaining \\(k\\) lines).\n\n   **Lemma 2.** Every point \\((x,y)\\in S_{2m+1}\\) belongs to at least one of the lines listed in (3).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Let \\((x,y)\\in S_{2m+1}\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   C=2x+y,\\qquad D=x+2y .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The numbers \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\le C,D\\le 4m+3,\\qquad C+D=3(x+y)\\le 3(2m+2)=6m+6 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence at least one of the inequalities \\(C\\le 2m+3+m\\) or \\(D\\le 2m+3+m\\) holds; otherwise we would have\n   \\(C>D>2m+3+m\\) and \\(D>C>2m+3+m\\), which together with (6) would give\n   \\(C+D>2(2m+3+m)=4m+6\\), contradicting \\(C+D\\le6m+6\\).\n\n   *Case 1.* \\(C\\le 3m+3\\).  \n   Then \\(C=2m+3+i\\) for some integer \\(i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le m\\).  \n   If \\(i\\le m-1\\) the point lies on the line \\(\\mathcal A_i\\).  \n   If \\(i=m\\) then \\(C=4m+3\\) and the only solution of \\(2x+y=4m+3\\) in \\(S_{2m+1}\\) is \\((2m+1,1)\\); this point also satisfies \\(x+2y=2m+3\\) and therefore belongs to \\(\\mathcal B_0\\).\n\n   *Case 2.* \\(D\\le 3m+3\\).  \n   The same argument, using the definition of the family \\(\\mathcal B_i\\), shows that the point lies on some \\(\\mathcal B_i\\) (or, when \\(D=4m+3\\), on the diagonal \\(\\mathcal C\\)).  \n\n   In every case the point is covered. ∎\n\n   Lemma 2 together with (4) shows that the \\(k\\) lines described in (3) cover all points of \\(S_k\\).  \n   Consequently, for every odd \\(k\\) there exists a covering of the required points by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **From the triangle to the whole problem.**  \n   For a given \\(n\\ge3\\) and a chosen admissible \\(k\\) we proceed as follows.\n\n   * If \\(k=0\\) we take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); they are non‑sunny and clearly cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(k\\) is odd, write \\(k=2m+1\\).  \n     Use the construction of Lemma 2 to cover the triangle \\(S_k\\) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n     The remaining points of \\(S_n\\) are those with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\); they are covered by the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\), which are non‑sunny.  \n     Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   Hence for every odd \\(k\\le n\\) a configuration exists.\n\nCombining the impossibility for even \\(k>0\\) with the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and for odd \\(k\\) yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The set of required points\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-1,\\;b'=b-1\\) we obtain the equivalent description  \n\n\\[\na',b'\\ge0,\\qquad a'+b'\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{t=0}^{\\,n-1}(t+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A bound for a sunny line  \n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line and denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(L)=|L\\cap S_n|\n\\]\n\nthe number of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nFor every sunny line \\(L\\) one has  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(L)\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor a point \\((x,y)\\in L\\cap S_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nx+y=(1+m)x+c .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\neq-1\\), the integer \\(1+m\\) is non‑zero; let  \n\n\\[\nd=|1+m|\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\]\n\nEquation (3) shows that the values of the sum \\(x+y\\) taken on the integer points of \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression with difference \\(d\\).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\); therefore the progression can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{d}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{d}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n\\]\n\ndifferent integers.  \nBecause \\(d\\ge1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(L)\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{1}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n        =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Even numbers of sunny lines are impossible  \n\nAssume that for some integer \\(k\\ge1\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_k\\).  \nUsing Lemma 1 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|S_k|\n   \\le k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, say \\(k=2m\\), then \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=m\\) and (4) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2m(2m+1)}2\\le 2m\\cdot m\n\\