            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The set of required points\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-1,\\;b'=b-1\\) we obtain the equivalent description  \n\n\\[\na',b'\\ge0,\\qquad a'+b'\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{t=0}^{\\,n-1}(t+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A bound for a sunny line  \n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line and denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(L)=|L\\cap S_n|\n\\]\n\nthe number of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nFor every sunny line \\(L\\) one has  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(L)\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor a point \\((x,y)\\in L\\cap S_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nx+y=(1+m)x+c .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\neq-1\\), the integer \\(1+m\\) is non‑zero; let  \n\n\\[\nd=|1+m|\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\]\n\nEquation (3) shows that the values of the sum \\(x+y\\) taken on the integer points of \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression with difference \\(d\\).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\); therefore the progression can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{d}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{d}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n\\]\n\ndifferent integers.  \nBecause \\(d\\ge1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(L)\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{1}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n        =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Even numbers of sunny lines are impossible  \n\nAssume that for some integer \\(k\\ge1\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_k\\).  \nUsing Lemma 1 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|S_k|\n   \\le k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, say \\(k=2m\\), then \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=m\\) and (4) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2m(2m+1)}2\\le 2m\\cdot m\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2m^{2}+m\\le2m^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For even }k>0\\text{ a covering by }k\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The case \\(k=0\\) is trivially possible: take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A covering for odd numbers of sunny lines  \n\nLet now  \n\n\\[\nk=2m+1\\qquad(m\\ge1)\n\\]\n\nbe odd.  We shall exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Three families of lines  \n\nConsider the following three families of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A_i &: 2x+y=2m+3+i\\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,m),\\\\[2mm]\n\\mathcal B_i &: x+2y=2m+3+i\\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,m-1),\\\\[2mm]\n\\mathcal C   &: y=x .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal A\\) have slope \\(-2\\), those in \\(\\mathcal B\\) have slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\), and \\(\\mathcal C\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore every line listed in (6) is sunny.\n\nThe line \\(\\mathcal A_m\\) (the one with constant \\(4m+3\\)) contains only the single point \\((2m+1,1)\\).  \nThat point also lies on the line \\(\\mathcal B_0\\) (since \\(x+2y=2m+3\\)).  \nHence we may **omit** \\(\\mathcal A_m\\) without losing any coverage.  \n\nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\bigl\\{\\mathcal A_i\\mid 0\\le i\\le m-1\\bigr\\}\n          \\cup\\bigl\\{\\mathcal B_i\\mid 0\\le i\\le m-1\\bigr\\}\n          \\cup\\{\\mathcal C\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\n(m)+(m)+1=2m+1=k\n\\]\n\ndistinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Every point is covered  \n\n**Lemma 2.** Every point \\((x,y)\\in S_{2m+1}\\) belongs to at least one line of the family \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nC=2x+y,\\qquad D=x+2y .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBoth numbers are integers and satisfy  \n\n\\[\n3\\le C,D\\le4m+3,\\qquad C+D=3(x+y)\\le3(2m+2)=6m+6 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence at least one of the inequalities  \n\n\\[\nC\\le3m+3\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad D\\le3m+3\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nholds; otherwise we would have \\(C>3m+3\\) and \\(D>3m+3\\), which together with (9) would give\n\\(C+D>6m+6\\), a contradiction.\n\n*Case 1.* \\(C\\le3m+3\\).  \nWrite \\(C=2m+3+i\\) with an integer \\(i\\) satisfying \\(0\\le i\\le m\\).  \n\n*If \\(i\\le m-1\\)*, then the point satisfies the equation of the line \\(\\mathcal A_i\\) and is covered.  \n\n*If \\(i=m\\)*, then \\(C=4m+3\\) and the only solution of \\(2x+y=4m+3\\) inside \\(S_{2m+1}\\) is \\((2m+1,1)\\); this point also satisfies \\(x+2y=2m+3\\) and therefore lies on \\(\\mathcal B_0\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(D\\le3m+3\\).  \nThe argument is symmetric: write \\(D=2m+3+i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le m\\).  \n\n*If \\(i\\le m-1\\)* the point lies on \\(\\mathcal B_i\\).  \n\n*If \\(i=m\\)* then \\(D=4m+3\\) and the unique point of \\(S_{2m+1}\\) with \\(x+2y=4m+3\\) is \\((1,2m+1)\\); this point also satisfies \\(2x+y=2m+3\\) and hence belongs to \\(\\mathcal A_0\\).\n\nThus in every case the point belongs to a line belonging to \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Completion of the construction  \n\nLemma 2 shows that the \\(k\\) lines listed in (7) cover **all** points of the triangle \\(S_k\\).  \nSince each of those lines is sunny, we have exhibited a covering of \\(S_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  From the triangle to the original problem  \n\nLet the given integer be \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy the condition of the theorem.\n\n*If \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  They are non‑sunny and clearly contain every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd, write \\(k=2m+1\\).*  \nApply the construction of Section 4 to the triangle \\(S_k\\); we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).  \nThe remaining points of \\(S_n\\) are those with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\); they are covered by the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nAll together we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and every required lattice point lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\n- For even \\(k>0\\) a covering by \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible (Lemma 1 and inequality (4)).  \n- For \\(k=0\\) a covering exists (vertical lines).  \n- For every odd \\(k\\le n\\) a covering exists (explicit construction of Section 4 together with the vertical lines for the remaining columns).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\